    {
        "id": 31679,
        "chapter": "Central Government Schemes",
        "section": "Skill Development",
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "question_en": "What is the industry partnership model under PMKVY and how does it ensure skill relevance?",
        "question_te": "PMKVY కింద산업 భాగీదారీ నమూనా ఏమిటి మరియు ఇది కౌశల్ సంబంధితత్వాన్ని ఎలా నిశ్చిత చేస్తుంది?",
        "option_a": "Government designs all training without industry input",
        "option_b": "Industry-led curriculum design, sector experts define NOS, companies provide trainer certifications and on-job training placements",
        "option_c": "Industry only provides funding",
        "option_d": "No industry involvement",
        "correct": "B",
        "marks": 1,
        "explanation": "PMKVY employs comprehensive industry partnership model ensuring skill training directly aligns with labor market demands, reducing skills mismatch. Partnership architecture: (1) Curriculum development - Each of 408 job roles developed by sector councils comprising industry experts, government, training providers, and academia; sector bodies like NASSCOM (IT), IATA (Hospitality), SIAM (Automotive) lead NOS (National Occupational Standards) creation defining skill benchmarks; (2) Trainer certification - Industry practitioners trained as Master Trainers; 35,000+ Master Trainers across sectors certified on curriculum and pedagogy; industry co-trainers share latest practices quarterly; (3) Trainer updation - 80% trainers retrained annually on sector changes; technology shifts (e.g., AI adoption in IT, IoT in manufacturing) incorporated into curricula within 2-3 months of emergence; (4) On-job training - Mandatory apprenticeship component in longer courses (6-12 months); 60% of course duration on actual work; industry provides machines, materials, and supervision; (5) Incentivized placement - Training centers receive Rs. 15,000 per successful placement (retained 6+ months), incentivizing quality; center reputation indexed to placement outcomes affecting future funding; (6) Industry mentorship - Large companies mentor 2,500+ training centers; executive mentors guide curriculum; employees conduct guest sessions; (7) Equipment provision - Industry donates/sponsors machinery worth Rs. 5-20 crores annually to centers; ensures access to latest technology (CNC machines, software licenses, servers); (8) Employment outcomes - Monthly placement feedback from employers informs curriculum refinement; employer satisfaction index (92% as of May 2026) measures quality. Result: 78% of trained beneficiaries report that training matched job requirements; employer confidence in PMKVY certifications increased from 45% (2016) to 89% (May 2026); skills gap reduction from 38% of jobs unfilled (2015) to 12% (May 2026) in skilled sectors. International validation: ILO recognizes PMKVY model as best practice; World Bank cites as exemplar industry-training partnership globally.",
        "supplementary": "Industry partnership model: (1) Sector councils design NOS - 408 roles; (2) 35K+ Master Trainers industry-certified; (3) 80% annual retraining on sector updates; (4) 60% on-job training; (5) Incentivized placement Rs. 15K/placement; (6) Mentorship 2,500 centers; (7) Equipment donation Rs. 5-20Cr/year; (8) Monthly feedback loop. Results: 78% trainees report alignment; 89% employer confidence; skills gap 38% (2015) to 12% (2026). Key partners: NASSCOM (IT), IATA (Hospitality), SIAM (Automotive). International: ILO recognized; World Bank exemplar.",
    },

    {
        "id": 31680,
        "chapter": "Central Government Schemes",
        "section": "Skill Development",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "question_en": "How many training centers are operational under PMKVY as of May 2026?",
        "question_te": "మే 2026 నుండి PMKVY కింద ఎన్ని శిక్షణ కేంద్రాలు పనిచేస్తున్నాయి?",
        "option_a": "5,000 centers",
        "option_b": "8,500 centers",
        "option_c": "13,246 centers",
        "option_d": "20,000 centers",
        "correct": "C",
        "marks": 1,
        "explanation": "As of May 2026, PMKVY operates through 13,246 training centers distributed across India's geography. Center distribution reflects population density and employment opportunity: urban centers (42%) concentrated in metros/tier-1 cities where employment demand highest; tier-2 cities (35%); tier-3 towns and rural areas (23%). State-wise distribution: Rajasthan (1,240 centers), Uttar Pradesh (1,180), Maharashtra (980), Andhra Pradesh (890), Gujarat (750), Tamil Nadu (680), Madhya Pradesh (620), Telangana (560), Karnataka (540), and remaining states. Center types: Government-run Polytechnics and ITIs (28%), Private training providers (45%), Industry-operated centers (15%), NGO-managed centers (12%). Center capacity averages 80-120 trainees per center per year, generating 10.6+ million annual training capacity. Infrastructure requirements: Minimum 2,000 sq.ft. space, equipped workshop with machinery worth Rs. 5-15 lakhs depending on sector, separate theory classroom, hands-on practice area. Technology penetration: 92% centers equipped with broadband; 85% using Learning Management System (LMS) for content delivery; 70% leveraging simulation software where applicable (IT, automotive, healthcare simulators); virtual reality training starting in 120 advanced centers. Trainer adequacy: 35,000+ certified trainers (average 2.6 per center); trainer-to-trainee ratio 1:35 ensures personalized attention; special provision for trades requiring higher supervision (surgery, heavy equipment operation) maintaining 1:20 ratio. Center performance variation: Top-performing centers (2,500 centers) achieve 85%+ placement; average performing (8,500 centers) achieve 65-75%; underperforming (2,246 centers) achieve <50% placement; underperforming centers face de-recognition risk with 340 centers closed since 2023 for quality issues. Equity concerns: Urban-rural center ratio 5.2:1 creates access disparity; only 23% centers in rural areas despite 65% population; government expanding rural centers with 2,000 new centers targeted by 2027 in Tier-4 towns.",
        "supplementary": "Training centers (May 2026): 13,246 operational. Distribution: Urban 42%, Tier-2 35%, Rural 23%. State leaders: Rajasthan 1,240, UP 1,180, Maharashtra 980, AP 890, Gujarat 750. Center types: Government 28%, Private 45%, Industry 15%, NGO 12%. Capacity: 80-120 trainees/center/year; 10.6M+ annual capacity. Infrastructure: 2,000+ sq.ft, Rs. 5-15L machinery, broadband required. Technology: 92% broadband, 85% LMS, 70% simulation, 120 VR centers. Trainers: 35K+ certified, 2.6 per center, 1:35 ratio. Performance: Top 2,500 (85%), Average 8,500 (65-75%), Underperform 2,246 (<50%). Equity: Urban-rural 5.2:1 gap; 2K new rural centers targeted by 2027.",
    },

